\section{What the variance buys, and what it costs}{

The gain falls as a subject learns, so early trials move the mean
further than late ones without any parameter saying so. Nothing else
in this package has a learning rate that changes within a subject
without a covariate to change it.

The cost is identifiability. \code{sigma0}, \code{sigmaD} and the fixed
observation noise \code{sigma_o} enter the gain only through ratios, and
\code{tau} trades off against the scale of the payoffs. Fitting all six
freely on a short session is not advisable; fix \code{sigma_o} (it is an
argument, not a parameter, for that reason), and consider fixing
\code{mu0} and \code{center} at the task's own center when the design is known.
}

\section{Exploration bonus}{

Not implemented. Daw and others compare softmax choice with rules
that add a bonus for uncertainty, and the filter here carries the
variance those rules need, so it is a short addition; it is left out
because a bonus and \code{tau} are hard to separate on the data sizes
these studies run. The state is in \code{\link[=frm_value_trace]{frm_value_trace()}} as \code{s1} to
\code{s4} if you want to look.
}

\section{The Laplace caveat}{

frmtmb integrates the subject effects out with a Laplace
approximation, which is exact only when the conditional log-density
is quadratic. For binary choices it is not, and the fewer trials a
subject has the less quadratic it is. Measured on this family, the
fixed effects survive short sessions and the variance components do
not: see the Laplace section of \code{vignette("learning")} and
\code{?frmtmb.learn} for the numbers. \code{frm(importance =)}, which is the
usual way to price that error, is REFUSED for every family here; the
refusal names the seam.
}

